1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,872
GPT-6.2 Astra model:

2
00:00:02,872 --> 00:00:04,234
you pick by price

3
00:00:04,234 --> 00:00:05,982
but how much text it uses

4
00:00:05,982 --> 00:00:07,253
actually sets your cost.

5
00:00:07,753 --> 00:00:08,675
Here's why:

6
00:00:08,675 --> 00:00:11,382
a taxi fare is mostly rate times miles;

7
00:00:11,382 --> 00:00:12,596
the route sets the miles.

8
00:00:13,096 --> 00:00:14,957
Before, you'd compare the rate:

9
00:00:14,957 --> 00:00:15,932
price per token,

10
00:00:15,932 --> 00:00:16,733
a chunk of text,

11
00:00:16,733 --> 00:00:18,334
assuming pricier meant better.

12
00:00:18,834 --> 00:00:20,237
Now Astra's price per token

13
00:00:20,237 --> 00:00:23,810
is at least 20% below GPT-6 Astra's.

14
00:00:24,110 --> 00:00:26,184
Yet on one work test, OpenAI says,

15
00:00:26,184 --> 00:00:28,966
it scored higher at under a third of the cost.

16
00:00:29,466 --> 00:00:31,948
Like a driver who picks the best route:

17
00:00:31,948 --> 00:00:34,615
OpenAI credits writing fewer tokens,

18
00:00:34,615 --> 00:00:35,702
plus lower prices.

19
00:00:36,202 --> 00:00:37,915
On one coding test, both at max,

20
00:00:37,915 --> 00:00:40,701
OpenAI says the cheaper model, Sol,

21
00:00:40,701 --> 00:00:42,535
nearly matched Astra's score

22
00:00:42,535 --> 00:00:44,034
at about a fifth the cost.

23
00:00:44,534 --> 00:00:45,974
So, what we learned:

24
00:00:45,974 --> 00:00:48,262
price per token is the taxi's rate;

25
00:00:48,262 --> 00:00:50,286
your cost is price times tokens:

26
00:00:50,286 --> 00:00:51,376
compare cost per task.

